<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ain-i-Akbari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ain-i-Akbari is a detailed account of the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, written by his minister Abu'l-Fazl and completed around 1598 as the third volume of his Akbarnama. It describes the court, army, revenue system and provinces, with statistics on crops, prices and local chiefs. It is one of the most important sources for 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari.jpg" alt="Ain-i-Akbari" width="320" height="540" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basawan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basawan_-_The_Young_Emperor_Akbar_Arrests_the_Insolent_Shah_Abu%E2%80%99l-Maali,_page_from_a_manuscript_of_the_Akbarnama_-_1919.898_-_Art_Institute_of_Chicago.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The title means the Administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. Written by Abu'l-Fazl in Persian, the work is a set of administrative reports somewhat like a gazetteer; the orientalist Heinrich Blochmann described it as the administrative report and statistical return of Akbar's government as it stood about 1590. It is divided into five books, each made up of numbered regulations, or ains.</p><p>The first book, the Manzil-Abadi, has 90 ains on the imperial household: the mint and its refining of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, the harem, the royal seals, the kitchen and its recipes, fruits, perfumes, carpets and painting, the artillery, the branding and feeding of horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and elephants, and labourers' wages and building estimates. The second, the Sipah-Abadi, deals with the servants of the throne, the military and civil services and the courtiers, and describes the working of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansabdari-system">Mansabdari system</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The third book, the Mulk-Abadi, is given over to the judicial and executive regulations, the new era of reckoning, the survey of the land and the finance minister's rent-roll. It explains in detail the land revenue system that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> organised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Raja Todar Mal</a>, known as Raja Todar Mal's Bandobast, and gives an account of the administration, revenue and tribal divisions of each of the 15 provinces, or subas, of the empire.</p><p>The fourth book describes the social condition and learning, above all in philosophy and law, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, who formed the bulk of the population and whose advancement the emperor saw as a guarantee of his realm's stability, with chapters on foreign invaders, travellers and Muslim saints. The fifth collects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s sayings and rules of wisdom, with an account of the author's ancestry and life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1855 Sir Syed Ahmad Khan finished an illustrated scholarly edition of the work and asked the poet Mirza Asadullah Khan Ghalib for a foreword. Ghalib instead wrote a short Persian poem chiding him for spending his talent on dead things and praising the sahibs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who then held all the ains of the world; Syed Ahmad Khan afterwards gave up an active interest in history. The Ain-i-Akbari was one of the first Persian texts put into English, in three volumes published by the Asiatic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>: Blochmann's of 1873 and Henry Sullivan Jarrett's of 1891 and 1896.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ain-i-Akbari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Faxian recorded pilgrims around 400, the Chinese traveller Xuanzang described them in the 7th century, a Rashtrakuta inscription of the late 7th or early 8th century is the last at the site, and Abu al-Fazl's <b>Ain-i-Akbari</b> mentions twenty-four rock-cut temples.</blockquote><a href="/work/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Many commanded armed kinsmen: the <b>Ain-i-Akbari</b> puts the zamindars’ forces at 384,558 horsemen and over four million foot, a local militia rather than an army.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first well-attested ruler, Jatba, is mentioned in Mughal sources and acknowledged Akbar's suzerainty, as recorded in the <b>Ain-i-Akbari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/deogarh-kingdom">Deogarh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
